Z||000000||Pretender||05-24-99||09:14 PM||||i got an auto shifter from the junkyard today and installed it with just a little frustration., i start the car up and go through the grears, worked very nicely but when i turned off the key it wouldn't rotate back far enough to get the key out, i'm guessing that the car still thinks it's in gear?..so i tried to turn the key with a little pressure and i felt it snap., the whole key is still in the ing. the car is completely torn apart and i have to have it running tomorrow night!!!!!please help!.<br>
Z||000001||ezsurf||05-24-99||09:49 PM||||Pretender,<br>I might be able to help. I had a similar experience with a Celica GT once. From what I understand, your main problem is that the key is broke off in the ignition. This solution should allow you to get your car started. Underneath the ignition key lock cylinder there should be a grove similar to that of a flat blade screw (this is what the ignition lock cylinder turns to start your car). You need to remove the ignition key lock cylinder. Once the cylinder is removed, you should be able to insert a flat blade screwdriver into this grove and turn the ignition switch and start the car. You may be able to save the lock cylinder if you can get the remainder of the key out. Otherwise, you may have to get another cylinder lock and key. If you are unable to get the key cylinder out by any other means, obtain an auto body dent puller and jam it into the key cylinder and turn to lock the threads into the cylinder. The slide hammer on the dent puller should allow you to easily rip the cylinder out. Since you have the key broke off in the lock, you may have to drill it out to serve as a pilot hole to get the dent puller started. This is a desperate measure, but it will work. Either way your looking at another key, lock cylinder or both (and maybe a dent puller as well). Good luck.
Z||000002||theogre||05-25-99||07:21 AM||||Avoid using a puller. you need to pull things apart anyway.<p>there is one screw retaining the lock but it rather well burried. you need a new lock of course. a steering wheel puller, and a lock plate tool. the whole bunch will run around 50$.<p>be carefull taking out the lock body. the contact for the key buzer is a bit delicate. you have to take it out of the hole before the lock comes out.<p>Your other problem is the park interlock cable. It's not adjusted right. it's adjusted on the shifer end. the ignition switch end has no adjustment.
Z||000003||Pretender||05-25-99||12:12 PM||||a steering wheel puller, and a lock plate tool....ok, what about the ing. thing not moving?...i think i bent i(where the key goes in)..is it easily bent back?...how do i adjust the park interlock cable?.. 
Z||000004||Pretender||05-25-99||01:20 PM||||ok i figured it out, the slack in the cabel is set by the little clip that goes up and down, i set it turned off the ing, and was able to pull out the entire key!..it was hanging on barely, any more pressure would of broke it off. i called the local dealership and there making me a set of keys for free!...well thanks for all your help!...:0)
